# global_reject.tkr — the §15 `global` REJECT host. Four illegal forms live one per namespace under
# src/, and the single failing build reports every diagnostic it finds, one per scenario below.
# `global` is a namespace-level fn/type/const modifier: never on a variable, never on a type member,
# and two globals sharing an exported signature (or a type name+arity) collide instead of overloading.

Feature: §15 reject — the compiler rejects the illegal `global` placements and the global collisions

  Scenario: r1_global_var — `global` on a variable binding (no mutable global state)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "`global` is not allowed on a variable"

  Scenario: r2_sig_collision — two `global` fns of the identical exported signature (ruling 3)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "duplicate `global` signature for 'h'"

  Scenario: r3_type_collision — two `global` types of the same name and arity (ruling 3)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "duplicate `global` type 'Dup'"

  Scenario: r4_type_member — `global` on a type-body method (globals are namespace-level only)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "`global` is not allowed on a type member"
